    (r"The metric may depend on coordinates of the augmented target, but it must be one smooth invariant metric on that target. Injectivity identifies the identity branch; it excludes neither distant competing minima nor later singularities.",
     r"The metric may depend on coordinates of the augmented target, but it must be one smooth invariant metric on that target. Injectivity identifies the identity branch; it excludes neither distant competing minima nor later singularities. With the law family as target and its Fisher metric, this is the edge alignment of \cref{comp:contextual-coupling} (whose target change is the inverse of the reference change used here). For noncompact groups an invariant metric is a real restriction: a stabilizer must act on tangent vectors through a relatively compact group, and even a free action can be non-proper, in which case no invariant metric exists. For the defining representation of $\GL(K)$, scalar matrices fix every conjugation link, so links alone never identify the group, and law or interface data must enter the metric."),
    (r"This curvature concerns changes of the link configuration across the contextual base. It exists with trivial graph backtracks and no agent cycle of length three. Normalized rotationally invariant laws can be attached without changing the calculation, because their infinitesimal orbit terms vanish.",
     r"This curvature concerns changes of the link configuration across the contextual base. It exists with trivial graph backtracks and no agent cycle of length three. Its values all lie along $\widehat e_3$, the common normal of the two axes, so the holonomy is Abelian; a non-Abelian link-only example would need more links. Normalized rotationally invariant laws can be attached, with a product metric on laws and links, without changing the calculation, because their infinitesimal orbit terms vanish."),
    (r"Conjugation cannot reveal a continuous gauge subgroup that fixes every input link. If a nonzero $Z\in\mathfrak g$ commutes with all admitted link fields, $h(c)=\exp(f(c)Z)$ leaves those fields and all their contextual derivatives unchanged. At a point where $f=0$ but $df\ne0$, affine covariance would nevertheless require $a'=a+Zdf$.",
     r"Conjugation cannot reveal a continuous gauge subgroup that fixes every input link. If a nonzero $\zeta\in\mathfrak g$ commutes with all admitted link fields, $h(c)=\exp(f(c)\zeta)$ leaves those fields and all their contextual derivatives unchanged. At a point where $f=0$ but $df\ne0$, affine covariance would nevertheless require $a'=a+\zeta df$. The same argument works pointwise for a selector that reads only link values and first derivatives: commuting with the link values at one point is enough to block covariance there."),
    (r"Even on a regular region, different column sets and target metrics can select different connections.",
     r"Even on a regular region, different column sets, weights, target metrics, auxiliary fiber metrics, and sector weights can select different connections."),
